course: dictionary
lesson: 10866
title: Victor, the winner
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A victor is the winner of a battle, a game or a contest. It is a formal word, so you will meet it more in history books and sports reports than in everyday talk.
explain: Look at the context: «Anyone who competes as an athlete does not receive the victor's crown except by competing according to the rules.» The clues are competes, athlete and rules, so the victor is the one who wins a contest that is played fairly.
explain: Two phrases to know are "the victor" and "emerge the victor". To emerge the victor means to come out of a contest as the winner. Winner means the same, but victor sounds more formal.
fact: A victor is the winner of a battle, game or contest.
fact: Victor is a formal word. Winner is the everyday word for the same idea.
fact: Emerge the victor means to come out of a contest as the winner.
fact: The context «does not receive the victor's crown except by competing according to the rules» shows that winning needs fair play.
exercise: What is a victor? | Someone who loses a game | A person who watches a race | A referee who makes the rules | *The winner of a battle, game or contest
exercise: «Anyone who competes as an athlete does not receive the victor's crown except by competing according to the rules.» What does this suggest a victor must do? | Hide from the referee | Stay at home and watch | *Compete according to the rules | Ask for the crown before the game
exercise: Which everyday word means the same as victor? | Loser | *Winner | Referee | Athlete
test: Which phrase means to come out of a contest as the winner? | Break the rules | *Emerge the victor | Shake the referee's hand | Lose the crown
test: Is victor a formal or a slang word? | *Formal, used in history and sports reports | Scots dialect | Slang, used on the playground | Gen Alpha slang
exam: Paul's words say the victor's crown is given only to someone who does what? | Cheats quickly | *Competes according to the rules | Wins without playing | Watches from the side
